course: dictionary
lesson: 520
title: Assure: making someone certain
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To assure someone is to tell them firmly that something is true, so they stop worrying. Badger says, «I assure you we shan't want any swords or pistols.» He is promising his friends something, so they can relax.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Badger says «I assure you» just before a promise, not a question. Someone who says it wants to be believed. So assure means to make someone feel certain, and it is often said to calm a worry.
explain: Assure can also mean to make sure something happens: «Practice assures success.» Practice does not speak, and it does not tell anyone anything; it guarantees the result. Check by swapping in your meaning: "Practice makes sure of success" still makes sense.
fact: Assure means to tell someone firmly that something is true, so they stop worrying.
fact: Assure can also mean to make sure something happens, as in «Practice assures success.»
fact: «I assure you» is a polite, formal way of saying "I promise you this is true."
fact: «Rest assured» means you can relax, because you can be certain.
fact: Assure is a verb: assures, assured, assuring.
exercise: «I assure you we shan't want any swords or pistols.» What does assure mean here? | Lose or misplace | Sell at a shop | *Tell firmly that something is true | Shout very loudly
exercise: Mum says, «Rest assured, the train will be on time.» What does she want you to feel? | Hungry and tired | Surprised by the sky | Angry and rushed | *Certain and calm, with no need to worry
exercise: «Practice assures success.» Which word could replace assures? | Forgets | Hides | *Guarantees | Interrupts
test: «I can assure you» is used before what kind of words? | *A firm promise that something is true | Loud shouts of warning | Questions asking for help | Goodbyes at the end of a letter
test: Which word is the past tense of assure? | assures | *assured | assuring | assure
exam: Badger says «I assure you» before a promise. Why does he say it? | To say goodbye politely | To make them laugh | *To make the others believe him and stop worrying | To ask his friends a question
